and it is an immense honor and pleasure  to stand here today  in this position it's not a position  that i expected to be in  some weeks ago uh however things  can change quite radically first of all  want to say thanks to almighty god for  giving me the health and strength to do  this  because i have my own challenges earlier  this year i won't pay particular tribute  to my wife  and to my family for the support that  they have been not just  over the course of the last number of  weeks but over the course of my  over 20 years in politics and i also  want to pay particular tribute and to my  election team  who have worked fantastically well and  have been on call  um virtually 24 hours a day worked  around the clock  to help secure this victory i'm looking  forward to  a positive relationship right across  northern ireland  and with my party colleagues out indeed  with people from other parties i think  that the opportunities for northern  ireland are great  the opportunities for us to make  northern ireland a great place  after this hundred years has passed and  we move into a new hundred years  our immense we have had great people in  northern ireland over those years  but the greatest thing in northern  ireland is the resilience of all of its  people  and from its outset it's had its trials  in the 1920s there were civil war  in the 1930s we had a depression in the  1940s there was a war  in 1950s there was another insurrection  by the ira  that started again in the late 60s and  worked its way right through  to 1994 and  throughout all of that our people have  shown  remarkable resilience and there's that  resilience that we  are going to go forward and make  northern ireland a great place  my father was a founder member of the  dep  some 50 years ago and i joined it after  the  death of the reverend robin robert  bradford mp  and at 1981 and throughout all of that  period  this party has been the authentic voice  of unionism and will continue to be the  authentic voice  of unionism under my leadership  the opportunities are great we want to  ensure that  for young people there is good jobs  available  in northern ireland educationally  we tackle the issues where um young  people from more deprived backgrounds  get greater opportunity want to increase  your university places  there is so much to do we have work to  do in our health service  which is greatly challenged at this time  with waiting lists which are horrendous  anywhere in the united kingdom so  there's much to do  there's much to be done and i stand here  today  very proud to be taking up the mantle as  leader of the democratic  unionist party and that brings with it a  responsibility to all of unionism  i want to say this very clearly i will  be  a leader in unionism who will be  reaching out to  other leaders in unionism i want  to see unionism working together  the northern ireland protocol has proven  to be a  massive challenge for us and if we are  to  fight this to ensure that everybody in  northern ireland  is not worse off as a consequence of the  protocol  then it's for us to do that together and  i want  to ensure that that is the case that we  don't have the unionist bickering  that we've had in the past and i will  encourage all unionists to work with me  to deliver an end which ensures we set  the foundations in this 2021  for another 100 years of northern  ireland within the united kingdom  thank you  the new leader of the democratic  unionist party  in northern ireland it couldn't be  closer could it david blevins was  telling us  it was on a knife edge this vote and  uh edwin puts the agriculture minister  got 19 votes so jeffrey donaldson the  other  contender got 17  there so 36 members of the party's  electoral college  voting uh you can see there david  blevins for us our senior  ireland correspondent so this was close  but we know  now who has edged it  yes they came remarkably close to a tie  which would have required the party  to conduct the entire leadership contest  all over again  but i think we can say two things as a  result of what we've just heard in the  last few minutes  one the dup is an almost equally divided  party between the pragmatic wing  it's representative in this contest  being sir jeffrey donaldson  and the ideological wing its  representative the victorious candidate  edwin pouch northern ireland's  agriculture minister he has a great job  of work ahead of him if he's going to  try to  reunite northern ireland's largest party  the democratic unionists  without further dividing the power  sharing government here at stormont  which is already wobbling  as a result of difficulties around the  northern ireland protocol  that brexit border in the irish sea i  think  the other thing that we have to make  very clear is that this marks  a seismic shift for the democratic  unionist party it is  an example of the grassroots of the  party taking back control if you like  from those who've been the top brass for  the last  decade one of the complaints that led to  the internal revolt against arlene  foster  was the fact that those who serve here  in the northern ireland assembly  felt there was a disconnect between them  to some degree  and those in the leadership of the party  and those who sit in  westminster for the party but many  people will be very very surprised that  sir jeffrey donaldson  a man who's been an mp for 24 years who  currently leads the party's westminster  team  and in his role as chief whip there  formerly was the man who negotiated that  one billion pound  confidence and supply deal with the  tories back in 2017  has not been victorious edwin putz  northern ireland's agriculture minister  a man who's held many different  portfolios here at stormont has just  edged it but he absolutely represents  that more ideological  more traditional more religious wing of  the dup and has in the past quartered  controversy  because of his strongly held views on  issues like evolution  and homosexuality
 

